   "subject": "RE: Quadratic relationship between latent variables",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Jonas—<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">     That’s actually about it.  You will square the indicators, and out of that extract a variance component that corresponds to the square of the factor.  The indProd function in the semTools package does a nice job.  Specify the set of three indicators for both the first set and the second set, and set match=T.  When you run the lavaan analysis, choose estimator=”MLR” because of the nonnormality of the products.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">     You read more about factor interactions than about quadratics, but the two track along side by side.  The most sophisticated approaches—Klein and Moosbrugger’s (2000) LMS method and Muthen’s QML/random slope approximation to LMS--do not square indicators, but they are not available in R yet, to my knowledge.  Often, though, the differences across methods are inconsequential.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">--Ed Rigdon<u></u><u></u></span></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lav...@googlegroups.com</a> [mailto:<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lav...@googlegroups.com</a>] <b>On Behalf Of </b>Jonas H<br><b>Sent:</b> Wednesday, December 03, 2014 4:46 PM<br><b>To:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lav...@googlegroups.com</a><br><b>Subject:</b> Quadratic relationship between latent variables<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hi, <u></u><u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I have a simple SEM model:<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><div style=\"border:solid #bbbbbb 1.0pt;padding:0in 0in 0in 0in;word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div style=\"border:solid #d6d6d6 1.0pt;padding:5.0pt 8.0pt 5.0pt 8.0pt;background:#fafafa\"><pre style=\"margin-bottom:15.0pt;line-height:15.75pt;background:#fafafa;border:none;padding:0in\"><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:black;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">model </span></code><code><b><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#666600;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">&lt;-</span></b></code><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:black;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"> </span></code><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">&#39;</span></code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><br></span><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">   # latent variables</span></code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><br></span><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">     L1 =~ x1 + x2 + x3</span></code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><br></span><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">     L2 =~ x4 + x5 + x6</span></code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><br><br></span><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">   # regression</span></code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><br></span><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">     L1 ~ L2</span></code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><br></span><code><span style=\"font-family:&quot;inherit&quot;,serif;color:#008800;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">&#39;</span></code><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;font-family:Consolas;color:#0a0a0a\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre></div></div></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br>So far so good. Now I would like to estimate the parameter for a quadratic relationship; Explicitly, I would like to have this regression:<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><div style=\"border:solid #bbbbbb 1.0pt;padding:0in 0in 0in 0in;word-wrap:break-word\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background:#fafafa\"><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\">L1 </span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">~</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\"> poly</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">(</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\">L2</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">,</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#006666\">2</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">)</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\"> </span></code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"><br><br></span><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#880000\">#or</span></code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"><br><br><code>L1 </code></span><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">~</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\"> L2 </span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">+</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\"> I</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">(</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:black\">L2</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">^</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#006666\">2</span></code><code><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;color:#666600\">)</span></code><u></u><u></u></p></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br>However, this syntax does not work in lavaan. How I can do that using the lavaan syntax?<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">My workaround would be to square the indicator variabales x4, x5, x6, but that seems a bit inelegant and I am also not completely sure whether this is equivalent.<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Any help is appreciated!<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Thanks,<br>Jonas<u></u><u></u></p></div></div></div></div>"
